Title: The Tell-Tale Heart
Credit: Written by
Author: Edgar Allan Poe
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/30/2026
Notes: One render medium throughout — period live-action, 1843. Candle and dark-lantern light, deep blacks, plaster and bare board.

FADE IN:

INT. BARE STONE CELL - NIGHT

A windowless stone cell. A single tallow candle stands on the floor. THE NARRATOR — a gaunt man of thirty, black hair damp against his forehead, collarless linen shirt, dark waistcoat, iron cuffs on his wrists — sits on a low stool, facing us. He is perfectly calm.

THE NARRATOR
True!—nervous—very, very dreadfully nervous I had been and am; but why will you say that I am mad? The disease had sharpened my senses—not destroyed—not dulled them. Above all was the sense of hearing acute.

He tilts his head, as though something moves under the flagstones.

THE NARRATOR
I heard all things in the heaven and in the earth. I heard many things in hell. How, then, am I mad? Hearken! and observe how healthily—how calmly I can tell you the whole story.

The candle flame leans. He leans with it.

THE NARRATOR
It is impossible to say how first the idea entered my brain; but once conceived, it haunted me day and night.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - DAY

A close, shuttered room. One blade of grey light across a heavy four-poster bed. THE OLD MAN — eighty, frail, thin white hair to his collar, long white nightshirt and grey woollen dressing gown — sits in a wing chair. His left eye is pale blue and filmed over; it does not move with the other.

THE NARRATOR sets a bowl of broth at the old man's elbow and tucks a blanket over his knees. Gentle. Attentive.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Object there was none. Passion there was none. I loved the old man. He had never wronged me. He had never given me insult. For his gold I had no desire.

The old man turns his head. The pale blue eye settles on the narrator and stays.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I think it was his eye! yes, it was this! He had the eye of a vulture—a pale blue eye, with a film over it.

The narrator's hands stop on the blanket. His face empties.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Whenever it fell upon me, my blood ran cold; and so by degrees—very gradually—I made up my mind to take the life of the old man, and thus rid myself of the eye forever.

INT. BARE STONE CELL - NIGHT

The narrator draws his stool an inch nearer to us. Confidential.

THE NARRATOR
Now this is the point. You fancy me mad. Madmen know nothing. But you should have seen me. You should have seen how wisely I proceeded—with what caution—with what foresight—with what dissimulation I went to work!

He smiles, pleased with the memory.

THE NARRATOR
I was never kinder to the old man than during the whole week before I killed him.

INT. UPSTAIRS LANDING - NIGHT

A narrow landing, bare boards, the old man's door shut. A clock below begins to strike twelve. THE NARRATOR stands with a dark lantern, all closed, closed, so that no light shows.

His hand takes the latch and turns it by the hair's breadth.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
And every night, about midnight, I turned the latch of his door and opened it—oh, so gently!

The door parts on a seam of blackness. He feeds the closed lantern through, then his head — slowly, very, very slowly.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Oh, you would have laughed to see how cunningly I thrust it in! It took me an hour to place my whole head within the opening so far that I could see him as he lay upon his bed. Ha!—would a madman have been so wise as this?

His thumb works the lantern's tin fastening a fraction. A single thin ray crosses the dark and lies upon the old man's sleeping face — upon the closed left lid.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I undid it just so much that a single thin ray fell upon the vulture eye.

The door. The seam of black. The ray. The closed lid. Again. Again. Seven times over, the same inch, the same hour, the same shut eye.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
And this I did for seven long nights—every night just at midnight—but I found the eye always closed; and so it was impossible to do the work; for it was not the old man who vexed me, but his Evil Eye.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - DAY

Morning. The narrator throws back a shutter and comes to the bedside, brisk and hearty, a shaving basin in his hands.

THE NARRATOR
Good morning to you! And how have you passed the night?

The old man smiles up at him and pats his sleeve. He suspects nothing.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
So you see he would have been a very profound old man, indeed, to suspect that every night, just at twelve, I looked in upon him while he slept.

INT. UPSTAIRS LANDING - NIGHT

The eighth night. The narrator's hand on the latch, moving so little that the movement cannot be seen at all.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Upon the eighth night I was more than usually cautious in opening the door. A watch's minute hand moves more quickly than did mine. Never before that night had I felt the extent of my own powers—of my sagacity.

A low chuckle escapes him before he can shut his teeth on it.

Inside, bedclothes shift suddenly. The narrator does not withdraw. He pushes the door on, steadily, steadily, into pitch darkness.

His head is in. His thumb slips on the tin fastening — a small bright scrape of metal.

THE OLD MAN (O.S.)
Who's there?

The narrator freezes. Nothing moves but the candle-shine in his open eye.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I kept quite still and said nothing. For a whole hour I did not move a muscle, and in the meantime I did not hear him lie down.

In the wall beside his cheek, a deathwatch beetle ticks. Tick. Tick.

From the bed, a slight groan.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Presently I heard a slight groan, and I knew it was the groan of mortal terror.

INT. BARE STONE CELL - NIGHT

The narrator sits very upright, hands quiet in their irons.

THE NARRATOR
It was not a groan of pain or of grief—oh, no!—it was the low stifled sound that arises from the bottom of the soul when overcharged with awe. I knew the sound well. Many a night, just at midnight, when all the world slept, it has welled up from my own bosom.

He looks aside, almost tender.

THE NARRATOR
I knew what the old man felt, and pitied him, although I chuckled at heart. He had been saying to himself—"It is nothing but the wind in the chimney—it is only a mouse crossing the floor," or "It is merely a cricket which has made a single chirp." All in vain; because Death, in approaching him, had stalked with his black shadow before him, and enveloped the victim.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

Black as pitch. The shutters are close fastened. Only the faint seam of the open door, and a head within it.

The lantern's crevice opens by a hair. A single dim ray, like the thread of a spider, shoots out and falls full upon the old man's face — upon the left eye.

The eye is open. Wide, wide open.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was open—wide, wide open—and I grew furious as I gazed upon it. All a dull blue, with a hideous veil over it that chilled the very marrow in my bones; but I could see nothing else of the old man's face or person: for I had directed the ray as if by instinct, precisely upon the damned spot.

Under the silence, a sound begins. Low. Dull. Quick.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
There came to my ears a low, dull, quick sound, such as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton. I knew that sound well, too. It was the beating of the old man's heart.

The narrator's jaw sets. The ray does not waver.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
It increased my fury, as the beating of a drum stimulates the soldier into courage.

He scarcely breathes. Sweat crawls at his temple. The beating comes quicker, and louder, and quicker still, until the plaster itself seems to carry it.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
But even yet I refrained and kept still. Yet, for some minutes longer I refrained and stood still. But the beating grew louder, louder! I thought the heart must burst.

The lantern trembles in his fist. Somewhere beyond the wall, a neighbour's shutter knocks in the wind.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
And now a new anxiety seized me—the sound would be heard by a neighbour! The old man's hour had come!

With a loud yell the narrator throws the lantern wide and leaps into the room. Light swings across the ceiling.

THE OLD MAN
(a single shriek)
Aah!

Once. Once only. The narrator drags him from the bed to the floor and hauls the heavy bedstead over on top of him. Then stillness.

He sits back on his heels in the wreck of the room and smiles gaily.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I then smiled gaily, to find the deed so far done.

Beneath the mattress, muffled, the beating goes on. The narrator waits, patient, head cocked. The lantern flame stands straight up.

The beating slows. Slows. A last stroke — and nothing.

He lifts the bed away and kneels to the body. He lays his hand flat upon the chest and holds it there, counting nothing, for a long while.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
There was no pulsation. He was stone dead. His eye would trouble me no more.

He reaches out and closes the pale blue eye with two fingers.

INT. BARE STONE CELL - NIGHT

The narrator lifts his cuffed hands, presenting them as evidence of his own cleverness.

THE NARRATOR
If still you think me mad, you will think so no longer when I describe the wise precautions I took for the concealment of the body.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - PRE-DAWN

Lantern on the floor. On the wall, the narrator's shadow at work with a saw, methodical, unhurried. A wooden tub set close beside him.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
The night waned, and I worked hastily, but in silence. First of all I dismembered the corpse. I cut off the head and the arms and the legs.

A chisel bites under a floorboard. Three planks come up. The dark between the scantlings takes what he lowers into it.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I then took up three planks from the flooring of the chamber, and deposited all between the scantlings. I then replaced the boards so cleverly, so cunningly, that no human eye—not even his—could have detected any thing wrong.

He beds the boards down and passes his palm across the joins. Nothing. He turns the lantern on the bare floor, then on the tub.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
There was nothing to wash out—no stain of any kind—no blood-spot whatever. I had been too wary for that. A tub had caught all—ha! ha!

A bell begins to sound the hour. Four o'clock — still dark as midnight.

On the fourth stroke, knocking at the street door below. Slow. Official.

The narrator wipes his hands on a cloth and picks up the lantern with a light heart.

INT. ENTRY HALL - PRE-DAWN

The narrator opens the street door. Three men in dark coats and tall hats stand in the cold: OFFICER MULLEN, broad, grey side-whiskers; OFFICER DACE, young, red-cheeked; OFFICER PRESCOTT, thin, a lantern of his own.

OFFICER MULLEN
Officers of the police, sir. A shriek was heard in the night, and suspicion of foul play; information has been lodged. We are deputed to search the premises.

The narrator smiles and stands wide of the door.

THE NARRATOR
You are welcome, gentlemen. The shriek was my own, in a dream. The old man is absent in the country. Search—search well.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I smiled,—for what had I to fear? I bade the gentlemen welcome.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - PRE-DAWN

The narrator leads the three officers up through the house and in at the old man's door, holding the lantern high for them. He opens a cabinet and shows them the old man's plate and coin — treasures secure, undisturbed.

Prescott's lantern crosses the mended boards and passes on.

In the enthusiasm of his confidence, the narrator carries in chairs and sets them about. He plants his own seat squarely upon the boards above the corpse and sits, at his ease.

The officers sit. They chat of familiar things. He answers cheerily.

OFFICER DACE
A raw night for it, sir.

The narrator laughs with them. Then, at the edge of hearing, a ringing begins.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
But, ere long, I felt myself getting pale and wished them gone. My head ached, and I fancied a ringing in my ears: but still they sat and still chatted.

He talks more freely, more brightly, leaning in. Mullen nods along.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
The ringing became more distinct:—it continued and gained definiteness—until, at length, I found that the noise was not within my ears.

Low. Dull. Quick. It is under his boots.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was a low, dull, quick sound—much such a sound as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton. I gasped for breath—and yet the officers heard it not.

Sweat on his lip. He talks faster, higher, his hands moving too much. Dace smiles politely.

The sound steadily increases. The narrator rises and argues about trifles, in a high key, with violent gesticulations.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Why would they not be gone?

He paces the floor to and fro with heavy strides, boots hammering the boards. The sound rises over the boots.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Oh God! what could I do? I foamed—I raved—I swore!

He seizes his chair and grates it hard across the boards — and still the noise arises over all, louder, louder, louder.

The three officers sit and chat pleasantly, and smile.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Was it possible they heard not? Almighty God!—no, no! They heard!—they suspected!—they knew!—they were making a mockery of my horror! But anything was better than this agony! I felt that I must scream or die!

He claps his hands over his ears. The beating swells until the lantern glass shivers with it.

THE NARRATOR
(shrieking)
Villains! dissemble no more! I admit the deed!—tear up the planks!—here, here!—It is the beating of his hideous heart!

He drops to his knees and tears at the boards with his fingers. The officers are already up, chairs going over behind them.

INT. BARE STONE CELL - NIGHT

The narrator on his low stool, hands in irons, candle nearly spent. He is quite calm again.

THE NARRATOR
They heard!—they suspected!—they knew!—they were making a mockery of my horror!—this I thought, and this I think.

The candle gutters. Under the flagstones, faint and patient — a low, dull, quick sound.

He tilts his head toward the floor and listens. He smiles.

THE NARRATOR
Hearken.

FADE OUT.

THE END
